var tafsirData_81 = {
  "1": "The opening verse of Sūrah At-Takwīr presents a terrifying image from the apocalyptic events that will overturn the entire cosmic order: “When the sun is put out.” The Arabic word for “put out” is kuwwirat, a term rich with visual force. It literally means to fold something up or to wind it into a coil, much like a turban is wrapped around the head. On that Day, the sun—this unimaginably vast, blazing sphere that has illuminated the world since its creation—will be folded and extinguished.\n\nIts light will be rolled up and taken away, plunging all existence into darkness. The use of the passive form “is put out” emphasises that this will happen by the direct command of Allah, without any agency of creation. The verse is part of a series of conditional clauses beginning with “when” (idhā), a construction that piles one cosmic upheaval upon another to build a scene of absolute catastrophe. This final dismantling of the sun directly answers the arrogance of those who felt self-sufficient.\n\nThe sun, upon which all earthly life depends, is not a deity; it is a creation that will obey its Lord’s command to fold and die. For the Meccan pagans who revered the sun and other celestial bodies, this verse is a shattering declaration that even the mightiest heavenly object is utterly subservient to the Creator. In the context of the preceding sūrah, which ended with faces bright and rejoicing or gloomy and dusty, the folding of the sun signals the end of all worldly light. The only light remaining will be the spiritual light of faith.\n\nThe image also reminds every believer that the comforts and certainties of this world are temporary. The sun, which rises and sets with such regularity that humans take it for granted, will one day be folded away, and with it, all worldly life. The verse thus serves as a powerful call to prepare for the Day when the familiar universe will be replaced by a new reality, and only one’s deeds will remain.",
  "2": "The second verse continues the unfolding of cosmic devastation: “and when the stars fall down.” The Arabic verb inkadarat means to fall, scatter, and become dim, losing their radiance. In the night sky, the stars appear as fixed points of brilliant light, symbols of guidance for travellers and objects of wonder for poets and thinkers. The pagan Arabs also worshipped some stars and used them for divination.\n\nOn the Day described in this sūrah, these celestial bodies will be plucked from their positions and cast down, their light extinguished and their orderly courses thrown into chaos. The image is one of a grand, terrifying unraveling of the heavens. The same stars that once adorned the sky and provided direction will now become part of the universal destruction. This verse works in tandem with the folding of the sun to completely dismantle the visible heavens.\n\nIt strips away any false sense of permanence. The early commentators mention that the falling of the stars refers to their scattering like dust, their light vanishing, and them being hurled into the ocean or into destruction. The precise nature is known only to Allah, but the effect on the human psyche is clear: the very framework of the sky will collapse. For the disbeliever who lived his life under the starry canopy and never reflected on its Maker, this event will be a horrifying shock.\n\nFor the believer, it is a confirmation that the Lord who set the stars in their orbits can and will dissolve them when their purpose is complete. The verse thus contributes to the surah’s larger theme of absolute divine power, reminding the heart that no matter how stable the universe appears, its continuity is entirely dependent on the sustaining command of Allah. When that command is lifted, everything falls.",
  "3": "Moving from the heavens to the earth, verse three declares: “and when the mountains are blown away.” The Arabic word suyyirat means to be made to travel, to be set in motion and then scattered as dust. In the Qur’ānic depiction of the Last Day, the mountains, which stand as the very symbols of stability and rootedness on earth, will first be shaken violently and then uprooted from their bases. They will be blown by the winds like clouds of dust, leaving the earth a barren, flat plain with no curves or elevations.\n\nThe human mind has always looked upon mountains as the ultimate embodiment of permanence; they are the landmarks by which people navigate and the fortresses to which they flee. The verse tears down that sense of security. The One who raised the mountains as pegs to stabilise the earth will command them to move, and they will obey, disintegrating into nothing. This is not a slow geological process but an instantaneous, catastrophic event that occurs at the blowing of the Trumpet.\n\nThe lesson is profound: if the mountains, with all their mass and grandeur, cannot withstand the command of their Creator, how can a human being, small and frail, stand before Him and refuse to submit? The verse also echoes the earlier surah’s theme of rejecting arrogance. The person who was indifferent and self-sufficient relied on the apparent solidity of his worldly standing. On that Day, every apparent solidity will vanish.\n\nThe blowing away of the mountains is both a physical event and a spiritual sign that all false supports will crumble. Believers are thus urged to anchor their hearts not in the mountains of this world—wealth, status, or power—but in the Ever-Living, the Self-Subsisting, who alone remains when all else is blown away.",
  "4": "With verse four, the focus shifts from the cosmic to a startlingly specific earthly detail: “and when pregnant camels are left untended.” The pregnant camel, referred to as al-‘ishār, was the most prized possession of the Arabs. A she-camel ten months into her pregnancy was a treasure of immense value, a source of future wealth, milk, and transport. People would guard these camels with the greatest care and would never abandon them.\n\nYet on this Day, due to the overwhelming terror of the events just described, even such treasured animals will be utterly neglected. Their owners will flee, leaving them without a shepherd, without food, and without any care. This verse paints a scene of total human collapse. The very things that people fought for, accumulated, and boasted about will become completely irrelevant in the face of the cosmic catastrophe.\n\nThe mention of pregnant camels also carries a specific cultural context: the Meccan pagans, to whom the Qur’ān was first addressed, prided themselves on their camels and their desert lifestyle. By singling out the most beloved type of livestock, the verse strikes at the heart of worldly attachment. The lesson is timeless: on the Day of Judgment, all forms of wealth and property that distracted people from their Creator will be abandoned. No one will think of their business, their herds, or their possessions.\n\nThe image also serves to magnify the horror of the Day. If a person will not even glance back at their most valuable asset, how terrifying must the event be? For the modern reader, the pregnant camel represents anything that holds supreme value in one’s life—investments, careers, properties. The verse is a sobering reminder that all of this will one day be left behind without a second thought, and the only asset that will matter is a sound heart and a record of righteous deeds.",
  "5": "The fifth verse presents another unprecedented event: “and when wild beasts are gathered together.” The Arabic word al-wuḥūsh refers to wild animals, beasts of the jungle and desert, creatures that normally live in fear of each other and of humans. The verb ḥushirat means to be gathered, herded, or mustered together. On that Day, a complete reversal of the natural order will occur.\n\nPredators and prey, which in this world are locked in cycles of hunting and fleeing, will be assembled side by side, utterly forgetting their natural instincts and enmities. The terror of the Day will be so pervasive that the lion and the gazelle, the wolf and the sheep, will stand together without the usual conflict. Scholars have noted several dimensions to this gathering. One is that it demonstrates the comprehensiveness of the resurrection; even the animals will be resurrected and gathered.\n\nAnother interpretation, supported by a well-known ḥadīth, is that after the judgment between humans, justice will even be meted out among the animals. The hornless goat will be allowed to exact retribution from the horned one that gored it, and then Allah will command them to become dust. This magnifies the concept of divine justice to an astonishing degree, showing that no act of aggression, even among beasts, is overlooked. The gathering of wild beasts thus serves as a powerful assurance for the oppressed that the Lord of all worlds will not let a single injustice pass.\n\nIt also intensifies the scene of cosmic upheaval. The human being who used to hunt or domesticate these creatures will see them assembled by a power far greater than his own. The verse strips away human dominance and reduces all creatures to the common state of being gathered before their Creator, reminding every soul that the true King is Allah.",
  "6": "Verse six introduces another catastrophic transformation: “and when the seas are set on fire.” The Arabic verb sujjirat has been interpreted in two primary ways by the scholars of tafsīr. One meaning is that the seas will be filled with fire, becoming a blazing, burning mass. The vast oceans that cover much of the earth, which in this world are a source of cooling water and life, will turn into a raging inferno.\n\nThe other interpretation is that the seas will overflow, break their boundaries, and merge with each other, becoming one single swollen ocean that floods the earth before being turned into fire. Both interpretations depict an unimaginable upheaval of the waters. The sea, which in the Qur’ān is a sign of Allah’s mercy and a source of sustenance, now becomes a manifestation of His wrath. This radical transformation reinforces the absolute power of the Creator over His creation.\n\nHe who placed the qualities of coolness and moisture in water can remove those qualities and replace them with fire. The event also carries a deep symbolic meaning. The seas represent the unknown, the vast, uncontrollable forces of nature that humanity has always feared and revered. By turning them into fire, Allah demonstrates that even the most overwhelming natural elements are His obedient servants.\n\nFor the disbeliever, this is a terrifying prelude to the Hellfire that awaits. For the believer, it is a call to reflect on the mercy contained in the ordinary order of the world, where water quenches fire and sustains life. That order is maintained purely by Allah’s will, and its suspension on that Day should inspire a sense of urgent preparedness. The verse thus serves as a vivid visual component of the apocalyptic panorama, merging the terror of the ocean with the terror of the flame.",
  "7": "After the cosmic and terrestrial upheavals, verse seven turns to the human reality: “and when the souls and their bodies are paired once more.” The Arabic nufūs (souls) and zuwwijat (paired, coupled, or joined) convey several layers of meaning. Most directly, it refers to the reuniting of souls with their physical bodies after the resurrection. The souls, which had separated from bodies at death, are brought back and paired with their original bodies, now recreated, so that each person stands complete for judgment.\n\nThis answers the Meccan skeptics who mocked, “When we are bones and dust, will we really be raised as a new creation?” It confirms that the same soul and the same body will be rejoined. A second, complementary interpretation is that souls will be paired with their companions of similar nature: the righteous souls with the righteous, and the wicked souls with the wicked, as alluded to in other verses where humanity is divided into categories. Both meanings enrich the verse.\n\nThe pairing of souls with bodies ensures accountability, as the body is the vehicle through which deeds were performed. The pairing of like with like ensures that one’s eternal company mirrors one’s inner state. The phrase also evokes the restoration of every individual identity. No one will be lost in the multitude; each soul will be precisely matched.\n\nThis verse marks a turning point in the sequence. The previous verses dealt with the destruction of the world; this verse and the following ones deal with the gathering and judgment of its inhabitants. It signals that after the great dissolution, a new creation will emerge, one in which every human being who ever lived will be present, fully conscious, and paired for eternity. The lesson is to ensure that one’s soul, in this life, pairs itself with truth and righteousness, so that its ultimate pairing may be with the blessed.",
  "8": "Verse eight initiates one of the most emotionally piercing passages in the Qur’ān: “and when baby girls, buried alive, are asked.” This refers to the horrific pre-Islamic Arabian practice of wa’d al-banāt, the burying alive of infant girls. This act was motivated by a combination of factors: fear of poverty, the perceived shame of having female offspring in a patriarchal society, and the concern about them falling into captivity and bringing dishonour. Fathers would dig a pit and bury their newborn daughters, or throw them into wells, while they were still alive and helpless.\n\nThe Qur’ān here does not merely condemn the practice; it dramatically elevates the victim to the status of a witness and prosecutor. The infant girl, who was given no voice in this world, is directly “asked.” This questioning is not to gain information, for the All-Knowing already knows; it is to expose the crime, to shame the perpetrators, and to give the victim a justice that was denied on earth. The mere mention of this scene brings the weight of eternity down upon the heads of those who committed such atrocities.\n\nIt also serves as a timeless indictment of all societies that devalue female children, whether through infanticide, selective abortion, or societal neglect. The verse transforms the helpless, buried girl into a figure of immense dignity on the Day of Judgment. Her voice will be heard, and her blood will cry out. The Prophet (ﷺ) and his companions deeply absorbed this teaching, and the practice was eradicated from Arabia.\n\nThe verse thus stands as a powerful testament to the Qur’ān’s revolutionary stance on human rights, justice, and the sanctity of every soul, regardless of gender or age. For the modern believer, it is a call to protect and honour the vulnerable and to know that every silenced victim will one day speak before the Lord of all worlds.",
  "9": "The question posed to the buried infant girl is made explicit in verse nine: “for what crime they were put to death.” The rhetorical nature of this question is a devastating indictment. A newborn infant has committed no crime; she has no capacity to sin, offend, or bring harm. The question exposes the absolute absurdity and evil of her murder.\n\nBy demanding to know “for what crime,” the verse reduces the perpetrators’ supposed justifications—fear of poverty, shame, or custom—to nothing. There is no valid reason. The girl is innocent, and her killing was pure transgression. The Arabic construction bi-ayyi dhanbin qutilat (for what sin was she killed) is deliberately open, leaving the guilty with no possible answer.\n\nThis question is not only a condemnation of the pre-Islamic Arabs but also a universal denunciation of any practice that views a human life as disposable based on gender, disability, or economic burden. In the context of the sūrah’s progression, this verse deepens the theme of accountability. The small, forgotten, buried body will be resurrected, paired with her soul, and given a voice that will thunder through the court of divine justice. For the believers, this is a source of immense hope: no wrong will remain unaddressed.\n\nFor the oppressors, it is a source of terror: their hidden crimes will be brought into the open. The verse also connects to the earlier rebuke of arrogance and self-sufficiency in Sūrah ‘Abasa. The same attitude that made some men consider their daughters a burden and bury them alive is born of a heart that is indifferent to divine guidance. The Qur’ān here restores the balance, placing the ultimate value on taqwā and innocent life, not on worldly notions of honour.\n\nIt is a verse that should stir every reader’s conscience and affirm that in Allah’s sight, every soul matters perfectly.",
  "10": "With verse ten, the sequence moves to the great public disclosure of human actions: “and when the records of deeds are laid open.” The “records” translate ṣuḥuf, the scrolls or pages upon which every deed, word, and even intention has been meticulously inscribed by the angelic scribes. The phrase “laid open” means nushirat, spread out, unrolled, and published for all to see. The private ledger of each person’s life, which in this world was hidden from others and often forgotten by the self, will be made manifest.\n\nNothing will remain concealed. This public disclosure is an integral part of divine justice. The disbelievers who mocked the idea of resurrection and accountability will find their entire life story laid out before them and before the assembled creation. The righteous will receive their record in their right hand, filled with good, and will rejoice.\n\nThe wicked will receive theirs in the left hand or from behind their backs, filled with evidence of their transgressions. The laying open of the records is a moment of absolute clarity. There will be no pleading, no bribes, no false witnesses. The text itself is sufficient.\n\nThis verse instills a profound sense of vigilance in the believer. The thought that every glance, every whisper, every secret thought is being recorded and will one day be laid open is a powerful deterrent against sin and a powerful motivator for good. It connects to the earlier surah’s reminder that the revelation is carried by noble, virtuous angel-scribes. Those same scribes are the ones who write the deeds.\n\nThe verse also echoes the scene of the questioning of the buried infant; justice demands that the records be opened so that no one can claim ignorance or deny their actions. The believer is thus encouraged to live a life that would not bring shame when the scrolls are unrolled before the Lord of all worlds.",
  "11": "Verse eleven returns to the cosmic upheavals with a striking image: “and when the sky is stripped away.” The Arabic verb kushitat means to be pulled off, peeled away, or removed, like the skin of an animal being stripped from its body, or a covering being yanked aside. The sky (al-samā’), which forms the majestic blue canopy over the earth and is described elsewhere as a protected ceiling, will be completely removed. The familiar heavens, with their stars, their rain clouds, and their sun and moon, will be torn off, exposing whatever lies beyond.\n\nThe complete removal of the sky signals the end of the earthly phase of existence and the beginning of the realm of the Hereafter. There will be no barrier left between humanity and the divine throne, no atmospheric comfort. The unveiling is absolute. For the disbelievers who looked at the sky and saw only a meaningless expanse or attributed its creation to blind forces, the stripping away will be a shocking revelation of the true Power behind it.\n\nFor the believers, this event is part of the promised transformation, after which they will see the angels and the grand realities that were hidden in the world. The image of stripping also suggests the removal of protection. In the Qur’ān, the sky is a guardian canopy; its removal indicates that the time of earthly protection and testing is over, and the time of direct accountability has begun. This verse adds to the comprehensive dismantling of the old order.\n\nThe sun was folded, the stars fell, the mountains were blown away, the seas were set on fire, and now the very sky is peeled off. Nothing of the familiar cosmos remains. It is the final clearing of the stage for the court of the Almighty, where the records are laid open and souls are paired. The believer’s heart trembles at the majesty of this scene, inspiring a life of constant readiness.",
  "12": "With the old world completely dismantled, the next reality is presented in verse twelve: “and when the Hellfire is fiercely flared up.” The Arabic is al-jaḥīmu su‘‘irat. Al-jaḥīm is one of the names of the Hellfire, describing a raging, intensely hot fire that consumes everything. The verb su‘‘irat means to be kindled, stoked, and made to blaze fiercely.\n\nOn that Day, Hellfire will not be a distant threat but a present, ferocious reality. Its flames will be intensified to a degree unimaginable in this world, and it will be brought forth for all to see. Some scholars mention that the stoking of Hellfire refers to its final preparation to receive its inhabitants, who will be the disbelievers and wicked sinners mentioned at the end of Sūrah ‘Abasa. The mention of Hellfire immediately after the stripping away of the sky creates a terrifying juxtaposition.\n\nThe familiar canopy that provided comfort is gone, and in its place, the unthinkable blaze of Hell is made manifest. This verse serves as the ultimate warning to those who lived lives of indifference and sin. The very fire that they denied or joked about becomes a visible, tangible reality. For the believer, the flaring up of Hellfire is a cause for immense gratitude for Allah’s mercy and a call to seek protection from it.\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) frequently prayed for refuge from the Fire. This verse reminds every listener that the universe is not being destroyed for nothing; it is being replaced by the abodes of recompense. Hellfire is a creation of Allah that will display His justice and wrath upon those who earned it. The description of it being fiercely flared up indicates that its intensity is not static but will be maximised for the Day of Judgment, ensuring that the punishment fits the magnitude of the crimes of disbelief and oppression that were committed in the world.",
  "13": "In immediate contrast to the raging Hellfire, verse thirteen presents the ultimate reward: “and when Paradise is brought near.” The Arabic for Paradise is al-jannah, the garden, and the verb uzlifat means to be drawn close, to be made nigh. After the terrifying scenes of cosmic destruction and the blazing of Hell, the merciful Lord brings the gardens of eternal bliss within sight of the righteous. This drawing near is an act of honour and reassurance.\n\nWhile the disbelievers are horrified by the approaching punishment, the believers see their eternal home coming closer. Paradise is not described as blazing or roaring but as something gently brought near, offering peace and welcome. This verse beautifully balances the sequence of the sūrah. The same sequence that includes the questioning of the buried infant girl and the flaring of Hellfire also includes the nearness of Paradise.\n\nIt shows that divine justice contains both punishment for the wicked and the most generous reward for the good. The drawing near of Paradise implies that the long wait is over, the trials are finished, and the moment of entry is at hand. The believers will experience a joy that makes them forget every hardship. In the context of the Meccan period, when Muslims faced persecution, poverty, and mockery, this promise was a source of immeasurable strength.\n\nThe vision of Paradise being brought near transformed their suffering into a journey toward an approaching delight. For every believer reading this, the verse is a reminder that the pains of this world are temporary, and the eternal joy is not just a distant concept but something that will literally be brought close. The believer should live in a way that makes Paradise a near reality, rather than a distant dream, by embodying the mindfulness and eagerness described in the previous sūrah.",
  "14": "The long series of “when” clauses, twelve in total, finally culminates in the main response in verse fourteen: “on that Day each soul will know what deeds it has brought along.” This is the grand conclusion, the purpose behind all the cosmic upheavals. The phrase “each soul” translates kullu nafsin, emphasising individual accountability without exception. The word “know” implies an immediate, undeniable, and complete awareness.\n\nThe soul will not need to be told; it will see its entire life record with absolute clarity. The deeds it “has brought along” are literally “presented” or “made present” (aḥḍarat). Every action, good or evil, will be right there, accompanying the soul as its inseparable companion. The cosmic signs—the folded sun, falling stars, blown mountains, abandoned camels, gathered beasts, flaming seas, paired souls, questioned infant, opened records, stripped sky, flared Hellfire, and brought-near Paradise—all lead to this single moment of comprehensive knowledge.\n\nThe universe was dismantled so that each soul might fully comprehend its own life. This verse connects powerfully to the end of Sūrah ‘Abasa, where faces were described as bright or gloomy. Here, the soul’s knowledge of what it has brought along is the direct cause of that brightness or gloom. The verse is both a warning and an invitation.\n\nIt warns that the ignorance and forgetfulness we cultivate in this world will vanish, leaving only the stark reality of what we have done. It invites us to live now with that Day in mind, constantly asking ourselves what we are sending ahead. The deeds we “bring along” are the only luggage permitted for that journey. This magnificent conclusion seals the first half of the sūrah, leaving the listener with a heart trembling between fear and hope, and ready for the affirmation of the revelation that follows.",
  "15": "With verse fifteen, the sūrah transitions from the depiction of the Day to an emphatic defense of the revelation itself. It opens with a solemn oath: “I do swear by the receding stars.” The phrase “I do swear” translates the Arabic lā uqsimu, a form of intense oath that means “I indeed swear” or “Nay, I swear.” Allah swears by elements of His creation to draw attention to their significance and to confirm the truth of what follows.\n\nHere, the oath is taken by al-khunnas, the receding stars. The word khunnas comes from the root khanasa, meaning to retreat, to hide, to draw back. This describes the planets that appear in the night sky, move in their orbits, and then seem to recede or disappear at certain times, or it may refer to all stars that are visible at night and hidden during the day. The five known planets—Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn—are the classic referent because they exhibit retrograde motion and periods of invisibility.\n\nThe oath draws the pagan Arabs’ attention to the very stars they admired and sometimes worshipped, but it describes them in a way that highlights their subservience to a higher law. They are “receding,” meaning they retreat at the command of their Creator. This oath prepares the mind for the declaration that the Qur’ān is no poetry or magic but the Word of the One who controls those stars. The choice of the receding stars is profound: they are beautiful, mysterious, and orderly, yet they are not deities.\n\nThey are signs of the precise order of the cosmos, an order that points to the Orderer. The believer is invited to reflect on the movement of the heavenly bodies as evidence of divine wisdom, while the disbeliever is challenged to see that the same Lord who regulates the stars sends down the revelation.",
  "16": "The description of the stars continues in verse sixteen with two further qualities: “which travel and hide.” The Arabic terms are al-jawār al-kunnas. Al-jawār means those that run, travel, or flow swiftly in their courses. This describes the observable motion of the planets as they traverse the sky against the background of fixed stars.\n\nTheir movement is smooth, silent, and unceasing, governed by laws that never falter. Al-kunnas comes from the root kanasa, which refers to a gazelle entering its hiding place, or a creature retreating into its den. This beautifully captures the appearance of planets when they seem to disappear, either by setting below the horizon or by entering periods where they are too close to the sun to be seen. They “hide” in their celestial dens, only to re-emerge.\n\nSome scholars also interpreted al-kunnas as referring to the stars that hide during the day, as they are still present but veiled by the sun’s light. The overall imagery is of a group of swift-moving, luminous beings that periodically withdraw and conceal themselves. This dual characteristic of motion and concealment is a powerful sign. The planets are not static idols but creatures that follow their appointed paths and then obey the command to disappear.\n\nThe pagan Arabs, who observed these stars with great interest, are being told that the very stars they look upon are not masters of their own destiny. They run and they hide by the decree of Allah. This oath, therefore, is not merely poetic; it is a theological argument. It silently asks: If these stars are so governed, how can they be gods?\n\nAnd if the One who governs them swears by them, then the message He confirms must be true. The verse instills a deep sense of the Creator’s majesty and the order He has placed in the universe, an order that mirrors the guidance He has sent down in the Qur’ān.",
  "17": "The third element of the oath is introduced in verse seventeen: “and the night as it falls.” The Arabic verb ‘as‘asa has been interpreted by the early linguists and exegetes in two primary ways. One meaning is “when it approaches” or “when it sets in,” describing the gradual drawing of the veil of night over the day. The other, opposing meaning is “when it departs” or “when it retreats,” referring to the latter part of the night that turns away before dawn.\n\nBoth interpretations are linguistically valid and accepted, and both add richness to the oath. If taken as the night’s approach, it emphasises the moment of darkness enveloping the earth, reminding humanity of the power that turns light into darkness. If taken as its departure, it emphasises the retreat of darkness, pointing to the hope of dawn and the cyclical nature of time. In either case, the night itself is a magnificent sign.\n\nIt is a covering that brings rest, a time for reflection and prayer, and a testimony to the fact that the world is not governed by chance but by precise alternation. The night “falls” or “retreats” at the command of Allah, never failing, never rebelling. The oath by the night connects beautifully with the earlier oaths. The stars travel and hide within the night; the night is their backdrop.\n\nBy swearing by the night, Allah draws the heart to consider the comfort of sleep, the mystery of darkness, and the opportunity for secret devotion that the night provides. For the Meccan pagans who divided their time between trading by day and socialising by night, this was a call to see the divine hand in the most familiar rhythm of their lives. The oath also sets the stage for the next element, the day as it breaks, completing the pair. The night and day are two of the greatest signs, and they are invoked to support the truth of the Qur’ān.",
  "18": "The series of oaths culminates beautifully in verse eighteen: “and the day as it breaks!” The Arabic verb tanaffasa means “it breathes.” This is one of the most poetic and striking images in the Qur’ān. The day is described as “breathing,” which refers to the first appearance of the light of dawn, the gentle exhalation of brightness that pushes away the darkness of the night.\n\nIt is as if the day, after the stillness of the night, takes a deep breath and begins to shine. The verb captures the sense of relief, hope, and new life that comes with sunrise. The breaking of dawn is a daily miracle that people often overlook due to familiarity. Yet the precise moment when the darkness cracks and the first rays of light appear on the horizon is an event of profound spiritual significance.\n\nIt marks the call to the Fajr prayer, the beginning of a new day of opportunity, and a sign of the resurrection when the dead will emerge from their graves. By swearing by the day as it breathes, the Qur’ān draws the listener’s attention to the life-giving power of Allah, who brings forth light from darkness just as He will bring forth life from death. This completes a four-part oath: the receding stars that travel and hide, the night as it falls, and the day as it breathes. Together, they encompass the entire rhythm of the heavens: the stars in their motion, the darkness that conceals them, and the light that reveals the world.\n\nThe oath is a solemn build-up to the assertion that follows. The listener is meant to feel the weight of these magnificent signs and then hear the declaration that everything they have just sworn by points to the truth of the message. The believer’s heart is inspired to witness the sunrise as a living affirmation of the Qur’ān’s divine origin, a daily miracle that speaks of the Creator’s power and mercy.",
  "19": "The series of oaths now yields its grand purpose in verse nineteen: “Indeed, this Quran is the Word of Allah delivered by Gabriel, a noble messenger-angel.” This is the jawāb al-qasam, the statement that the oaths were taken to confirm. The word “Indeed” (inna) carries the emphasis of absolute truth. The Qur’ān is here declared to be the speech of Allah Himself, conveyed through an intermediary of the highest rank.\n\nThe phrase “a noble messenger-angel” translates rasūlin karīm. It is critical to understand that this “messenger” is Jibrīl (Gabriel), the angel entrusted with revelation, not the Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ). The next verses will separately address the Prophet’s character. The description of Jibrīl as “noble” (karīm) indicates his inherent honour, generosity, and elevated status.\n\nHe is noble in his nature, in his actions, and in the sight of his Lord. The verse refutes the pagans’ accusations that the Qur’ān was the invention of a poet, a soothsayer, or brought by devils. It states clearly that the source of the revelation is the Lord of the worlds, and the carrier is an angel of supreme nobility. This affirmation restores the dignity of the message after the Meccans’ mockery.\n\nIt also connects back to Sūrah ‘Abasa, where the revelation was described as being on honoured pages by the hands of noble and virtuous angel-scribes. The consistency between the surahs reinforces the divine origin of the Qur’ān. For the listener, this verse demands a posture of reverence. The words being recited are not human composition; they are the Word of Allah, delivered by a noble angel.\n\nThis should inspire awe, attentiveness, and obedience. The declaration also serves to defend the Prophet against the charge of madness, which will be addressed directly in the following verse. The emphasis on the nobility of the messenger-angel contrasts sharply with the baseness of the devils whom the pagans claimed were the source.",
  "20": "Verse twenty elaborates on the sublime attributes of the angelic messenger Jibrīl, stating that he is “full of power, held in honour by the Lord of the Throne.” The phrase “full of power” translates dhī quwwatin, possessor of strength. Jibrīl’s power is immense and awe-inspiring. He is the angel who, at Allah’s command, lifted up entire cities and destroyed the people of Lūṭ; he is the one who brought down the revelation with precision and force.\n\nHis strength is not fragile or limited but absolute in carrying out divine commands. The second phrase, “held in honour by the Lord of the Throne,” translates ‘inda dhī al-‘arshi makīn. The Lord of the Throne is Allah, and the mention of the Throne emphasises His supreme sovereignty over all creation. To be makīn near Him means to be established in a position of high rank, firm authority, and secure honour.\n\nJibrīl is not an outcast or a rebellious spirit; he enjoys a status of complete trust and favour with the Almighty. This double description—power and honour—shatters the pagan notion that the revelation could be brought by a shayṭān. The devils are powerless and accursed; Jibrīl is powerful and honoured. The verse implicitly argues that a revelation carried by such a being cannot be a product of madness or deceit.\n\nThe mention of the Throne also connects the earthly recitation to the highest realm of divine authority. The Qur’ān originates from the Lord of the Throne and is carried by an angel who is honoured right there at the Throne. The chain of transmission is pure and exalted. This description continues to build the believer’s confidence in the message, while serving as a warning to those who trivialise it.\n\nThe angel who brought the Qur’ān is not to be trifled with; the message he carries bears the weight of the Throne itself. The verses that follow will complete this defense of the revelation and address the Prophet’s own character and experience.",
  "21": "Having described Jibrīl as full of power and held in honour by the Lord of the Throne, verse twenty-one completes this portrait with two further qualities: he is “obeyed there in heaven, and trustworthy.” The phrase “obeyed there” translates muṭā‘in thamma. Jibrīl is not an isolated figure; he is a leader among the angels, one whose commands are heeded by the heavenly host. When he conveys an order from Allah, the other angels obey him without hesitation.\n\nThe word “there” (thamma) refers to the celestial realm, the heavens, the locus of pure obedience where no rebellion exists. This emphasises that the revelation was not brought by a rogue or isolated spirit but by a commander surrounded by obedient angels, all of whom recognise his authority. The second attribute, “trustworthy” (amīn), is of immense importance. Jibrīl is absolutely faithful in delivering the divine message.\n\nHe does not add, subtract, alter, or betray the trust placed in him. He brought the Qur’ān exactly as he received it from the Preserved Tablet. The combination of being obeyed and being trustworthy refutes any possibility of distortion in the chain of revelation. The Meccans claimed the Prophet was taught by a devil or a mere human; the Qur’ān responds that the true carrier is a mighty, obeyed, trustworthy angel.\n\nThis description also subtly links to the Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ), who was himself known among his people as al-Amīn, the trustworthy. Just as the angelic messenger is amīn, so too is the human messenger, and both are absolutely reliable in the transmission of Allah’s words. The verse builds a bridge from the heavenly reality to the earthly, preparing for the next verse which directly addresses the character of the Prophet. For the believer, this is a reassurance that the scripture they hold is not a corrupted text but the pure word of Allah, carried by an angel of flawless integrity, establishing a foundation of trust that demands a response of faith and action.",
  "22": "With the identity and integrity of the angelic messenger firmly established, the sūrah now turns to defend the human messenger in verse twenty-two: “And your fellow man is not insane.” The phrase “your fellow man” translates ṣāḥibukum, which means your companion, your associate, the one who has lived among you and whom you know intimately. This is a powerful appeal to the direct, personal knowledge of the Quraysh. They had known Muḥammad (ﷺ) for forty years before his prophethood.\n\nThey called him al-Amīn, the trustworthy. He had never shown any sign of mental illness, deception, or delusion. He was known for his sound judgment, honesty, and balanced character. So the accusation of insanity (jinnah) was not only false but absurd given their long experience of him.\n\nThe verse uses the third person “he” but the address “your fellow man” makes it direct and personal. It strips away the hostility and invites the disbelievers to rely on their own firsthand knowledge rather than the slander they spread. This defense of the Prophet also consoles him. The revelation reassures him that Allah Himself bears witness to his sanity and uprightness.\n\nThe charge of madness was a common weapon used against prophets, levelled at Nūḥ, Mūsā, and others, because the truth they brought seemed so radical that those who rejected it could only explain it as madness. The verse dismisses that claim with the simplest, most unassailable argument: you know this man. The implication is that if they truly examined their own experience, they would admit that he is not insane, and therefore the message he brings deserves serious consideration. This verse also serves as a timeless principle: the moral character of a messenger is a crucial testimony to the truth of his message, and the Prophet’s life was an open book of integrity.",
  "23": "Verse twenty-three provides a specific proof of the Prophet’s truthfulness and the authenticity of his experience: “And he did see that angel on the clear horizon.” The Arabic uses the particle laqad for emphasis, confirming that this vision is an established fact. The “angel” referred to is Jibrīl, and the “clear horizon” is the bright, visible horizon of the sky. The Prophet saw Jibrīl in his true, magnificent form on two notable occasions.\n\nThe first was at the very beginning of revelation in the cave of Ḥirā’, when Jibrīl appeared filling the horizon with his immense form, and the Prophet was overwhelmed. The second was during the Night Journey and Ascension (al-Isrā’ wa al-Mi‘rāj), where the Prophet saw Jibrīl in his original angelic shape near the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary. The mention of the “clear horizon” evokes the visible, undeniable nature of this experience. It was not a dream, a hallucination, or a whisper in the soul; it was an external, physical vision that occurred in a specific place and time.\n\nThe horizon being “clear” (mubīn) indicates that there was no ambiguity or hiddenness. This verse refutes the pagans’ claim that the Prophet was deluded or that his revelation came from obscure, hidden sources. The source was seen openly, on the bright edge of the sky. It also serves to confirm that the Prophet is an eyewitness to the heavenly realities he conveys; he is not narrating hearsay but reporting what he himself witnessed.\n\nFor the believers, this strengthens faith in the unseen world and in the Prophet’s direct connection to it. It demonstrates that the gap between the earthly and the heavenly was bridged, and the Prophet saw the noble messenger with his own eyes. This vision is a gift of certainty that fortified the Prophet for his mission.",
  "24": "The defense of the Prophet’s character and integrity continues in verse twenty-four: “and he does not withhold what is revealed to him of the unseen.” The Arabic phrase is wa mā huwa ‘alā al-ghaybi bi-ḍanīn. The key word ḍanīn has been interpreted in two authentic ways. One reading is that he is not stingy or withholding; he does not keep back any part of the revelation.\n\nThe second reading is that he is not a suspect or a person of doubt concerning the unseen; he is not suspicious about what he receives. Both meanings are complementary and powerful. The first meaning refutes the accusation that the Prophet might have concealed certain revelations out of fear or favouritism. He delivered the entire message, the warnings and the glad tidings, without holding anything back.\n\nThis is a vital point because the Prophet was entrusted with the unseen knowledge of Paradise, Hell, and the events of the Day of Judgment. He could have omitted the difficult verses that criticised the pagan ways to appease his tribe, but he did not. The second meaning, that he is not suspicious or uncertain about the unseen, confirms his absolute faith in the revelation. He did not doubt the truth of what Jibrīl brought him; he received it with complete conviction.\n\nThe “unseen” (al-ghayb) is all that is hidden from human senses—the angels, the Day of Judgment, the realities of the grave, the divine decree. The Prophet is not a reluctant or uncertain transmitter; he is a fully trusting and completely honest conveyor. This verse completes the picture of the faithful messenger. The angelic messenger was trustworthy, and the human messenger is equally trustworthy in receiving and delivering.\n\nFor the believers, this is a source of confidence that the Qur’ān they have is complete and unaltered, a perfect trust from Allah.",
  "25": "Having established the nobility of Jibrīl and the integrity of the Prophet, verse twenty-five delivers a direct blow to one of the main accusations of the Meccans: “And this Quran is not the word of an outcast devil.” The pagans sometimes claimed that the Qur’ān was the speech of a shayṭān or that it was a form of soothsaying inspired by devils who eavesdropped on the heavens. This verse categorically denies that. The word “outcast” translates rajīm, meaning one who is pelted with stones, driven away from divine mercy, and expelled from the heavenly realm.\n\nThe devils are precisely the opposite of the honoured, powerful, obeyed, trustworthy angel Jibrīl. They are cursed, weak, and deceitful. The verse establishes an unbridgeable contrast: the carrier of the Qur’ān is an angel of high rank and purity, so the message itself can only be pure and true. The devils, on the other hand, are barred from the heavens and cannot eavesdrop on the divine decrees, as other sūrahs explain.\n\nTherefore, they have no access to such a sublime message. The Qur’ān is the Word of Allah, delivered by an honoured angel to an honest prophet; it has nothing to do with the whispers of the outcast. This verse also has a deeper theological implication: the message of Islam leads to righteousness, worship of the One God, moral purity, and justice. The devils would never inspire a message that condemns them, calls to their enemy, and guides people away from their path.\n\nThe very content of the Qur’ān is proof against its satanic origin. For the believer, this verse is a call to appreciate the pure lineage of the revelation. It descended from the Lord of the Throne, through a noble angel, to a trustworthy prophet, and it remains a healing and guidance for humanity, free from any taint of darkness. This clear refutation should settle any doubt about the divine source of the scripture.",
  "26": "After the powerful refutation of all false accusations against the Prophet and the Qur’ān, verse twenty-six asks a searching rhetorical question: “So what other path would you take?” The Arabic fa-ayna tadhhabūn literally means “Where then are you going?” It is a question that confronts the disbelievers with the absurdity of their position. After the truth has been made so clear—the cosmic signs, the Day of Judgment, the integrity of the messenger, the nobility of the angel, the purity of the revelation—what alternative do they really have?\n\nWhat path can they possibly take that leads anywhere but to the truth? The question implies that all other paths are dead ends, misguidance, and darkness. The clarity of the Qur’ān has left no room for a reasonable excuse. The verse uses the present tense, indicating the ongoing wandering of those who reject the message.\n\nIt is as if the sūrah places the listener at a crossroads, where one path is illuminated by the divine word, and all other paths are shrouded in ignorance and destruction. This verse also serves as a turning point, shifting from defense to invitation. It challenges the listener to make a conscious decision. The pagans who heard this were left with no logical escape; they could not discredit the Prophet, they could not discredit the angel, they could not discredit the Qur’ān.\n\nSo where would they go? The same question reverberates through time. Every human being who hears the message and recognises its truth is faced with this inquiry: if you turn away from the Qur’ān, where will you go? To materialism, to empty philosophies, to man-made systems that lead to despair?\n\nThe verse is both a warning and a compassionate plea. It reveals the futility of rejection and the clarity of the Straight Path. It calls the soul to stop wandering and to walk towards the undeniable light that has been set before them.",
  "27": "The surah now states the very nature and purpose of the Qur’ān in verse twenty-seven: “Surely this Quran is only a reminder to the whole world.” The word “surely” (in) adds emphasis, and “only” (illā) signifies exclusivity, meaning its essential function is to serve as a reminder. The word “reminder” (dhikr) once again highlights that the Qur’ān is not bringing a strange or new message; it is awakening the primordial knowledge of the Creator that is already etched into the human soul. It is a reminder of the covenant, of the purpose of life, of the Day of meeting the Lord, of the moral law.\n\nThe scope of this reminder is universal: “to the whole world” (li’l-‘ālamīn). This includes all of humanity and the jinn, across all times and places. The Meccan pagans were mistaken if they thought the message was only for them or that they could determine its worth. The Qur’ān is a global and timeless call.\n\nThis verse also counters the earlier arrogance of those who felt self-sufficient and indifferent. The reminder is not for the elite or the powerful alone; it is for all creation. The universality of the Qur’ān means that every person, regardless of race, status, or era, can access its guidance. This elevates the responsibility of the believers to convey the message and the responsibility of every individual to heed it.\n\nThe phrase “only a reminder” also corrects any misconception that the Prophet was a tyrant forcing people or a soothsayer predicting arbitrary events. He was simply a warner and a bearer of a reminder. The reminder is sufficient for those who have hearts. The verse thus connects back to the earlier sūrahs and within this sūrah, emphasising that the purpose of the cosmic signs and the revelation is to awaken.\n\nFor the modern reader, the verse reaffirms that the Qur’ān remains as relevant today as it was at its revelation, a living reminder for a world that constantly forgets its Creator.",
  "28": "Verse twenty-eight narrows the focus of the universal reminder to individual responsibility: “to whoever of you wills to take the Straight Way.” The phrase “whoever of you” (li-man shā’a minkum) places the choice squarely in the hands of each person. The reminder is available, clear, and accessible; now it is up to the individual to will to take the Straight Way. The “Straight Way” (al-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm) is the path of Islam, the path of submission to Allah, the path that leads to His pleasure and to Paradise.\n\nIt is straight because it is free of crookedness, confusion, and deviation. It is the direct route to the destination of eternal happiness. The verse affirms human free will and accountability. The Qur’ān does not compel belief; it presents the truth and invites the will.\n\nThe phrase “whoever wills” also contains a sense of dignity and honour. Allah has given human beings the capacity to choose, and He addresses them as beings capable of making a conscious, meaningful decision. This is a profound affirmation of human agency. Yet the verse also implies that merely wanting is not enough; one must take active steps to walk the Straight Way.\n\nIt is a combination of sincere intention and righteous action. This verse connects back to the opening of the sūrah, where the cosmic upheavals were described. Faced with that inevitable Day, the only sane response is to will to take the Straight Way now. The reminder is presented, the choice is offered, and the consequences are known.\n\nThe believer’s heart responds to this verse with a renewed determination to stay on the path, while the seeker is encouraged that the door is open. The verse serves as a beautiful summary of the sūrah’s call: after all the terrifying warnings and the clear proofs, the mercy of Allah shines through in the simple, open invitation to walk the path of guidance.",
  "29": "The sūrah concludes with a profound theological statement that perfectly balances the affirmation of human choice in the previous verse. Verse twenty-nine declares: “But you cannot will to do so, except by the Will of Allah, the Lord of all worlds.” The word “but” introduces a necessary condition. The human being may will to take the Straight Way, but even that volition is not an independent power; it is entirely dependent on the will of Allah.\n\nThis is the doctrine of divine decree in its most beautiful formulation. Human beings have real will and real choice, but that will is encompassed by and operates within the overarching will of the Creator. No one can will to believe, move, or do anything unless Allah has willed to grant them the ability and the permission. This verse safeguards against arrogance.\n\nA person cannot boast, “I chose the Straight Path by my own intelligence and power,” because their very choice was made possible by Allah. At the same time, the verse does not negate responsibility, because the previous verse commands to will and to walk. The two verses together present the balanced Islamic position: full reliance on Allah while exerting one’s utmost effort. The phrase “the Lord of all worlds” (Rabbu’l-‘Ālamīn) reminds that Allah’s will governs not just human beings but all creation.\n\nThe Lord who folds the sun, scatters the stars, and brings Paradise near is the same Lord who guides hearts and enables wills. This final verse is a source of immense comfort and humility for the believer. It teaches them to constantly seek Allah’s help in staying on the Straight Path, as they recite in Sūrah al-Fātiḥah, “Guide us to the Straight Path.” The human will is weak and prone to fluctuation, but the one who attaches their will to the will of the Lord of all worlds finds stability and success.\n\nThe surah thus ends with the most essential truth: all power and will belong to Allah, and to Him is the ultimate return."
};
